<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-diagonalization-determine-similar">

<title>Determining similarity</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	determine whether the pair of matrices are <term><xref ref="definition-diagonalization-similar" text="custom">similar</xref></term>.
	If they are,
	exhibiting a specific <xref ref="definition-diagonalization-transition-matrix" text="title" /> that achieves a similarity relationship between the two.
	If they are not,
	provide convincing reasoning to justify this answer based on facts from <xref ref="section-diagonalization-theory" />.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		The matrices
		from <xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-2x2" text="type-local" />
		and <xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-1x1-J-1x1" text="type-local" />.
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		Not similar.
		<em>(Reasoning omitted.)</em>
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		The matrices
		from <xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-1x1-J-1x1-J-1x1" text="type-local" />
		and <xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-S-J-1x1-1x1-J-1x1" text="type-local" />.
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		Not similar.
		<em>(Reasoning omitted.)</em>
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		The matrix
		from <xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-1x1-J-1x1-J-1x1" text="type-local" />
		along with the matrix below.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				- 7 \amp  4 \amp - 7 \\
				-18 \amp 10 \amp -12 \\
				  8 \amp -4 \amp   8
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
	</p></statement>

	<hint><p>
		Is the second matrix diagonalizable?
		To what diagonal form matrix is it similar?
	</p></hint>

	<solution>

		<p> Yes, they are similar. </p>

		<p>
		Let <m> A </m> represent the matrix from
		<xref ref="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-1x1-J-1x1-J-1x1" text="type-local" />.
		In the answer to that exercise we provided a transition matrix and corresponding diagonal form:
		<md>
			P =
			\begin{bmatrix}
				0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
				1 \amp 0 \amp 2
			\end{bmatrix}
			\quad\implies\quad
			\inv{P} A P = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ \amp 4 \\ \amp \amp 6 \end{bmatrix}
		</md>.
		</p>

		<p>
		Let <m> B </m> represent the second matrix provided in this exercise.
		It can be determined that <m> B </m> is also diagonalizable,
		and following <xref ref="procedure-diagonalization-concepts-diag" /> leads to a transition matrix and corresponding diagonal form:
		<md>
			Q =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				1 \amp  1 \amp  2 \\
				2 \amp  1 \amp  3 \\
				0 \amp -1 \amp -2
			\end{abmatrix}
			\quad\implies\quad
			\inv{Q} B Q = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ \amp 4 \\ \amp \amp 6 \end{bmatrix}
		</md>.
		</p>

		<p>
		So <m> A </m> and <m> B </m> are similar to the same diagonal form,
		and from that we have <md> \inv{Q} B Q = \inv{P} A P </md>.
		Isolating <m> B </m> in this equality leads to similarity relation <m> B = \inv{R} A R </m>
		for transition matrix <m> R = P \inv{Q} </m>.
		</p>

	</solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
